diagnosis_text:
I inspected the OSPF stub area behind R2 (Area 4) and found no evidence of a “route-summarization loop” or a bad default being leaked into the backbone.

Evidence:
- On R10 (the internal router in Area 4), the routing table shows the expected OSPF default route only toward the ABR (O>* 0.0.0.0/0 via 21.1.1.1, eth0). This default is originated by the ABR as required in a stub area.
- On R2 (the ABR), there is no default route installed (show ip route 0.0.0.0/0 json returns empty). Its OSPF database confirms it originates the default only inside Area 4 (show ip ospf database summary 0.0.0.0 adv-router 23.1.1.1 shows the LSA only under Area 0.0.0.4 [Stub]).
- Backbone routers (R1, R3) also have no default route installed, so no 0/0 is circulating in Area 0.
- R2 correctly advertises Area 4’s internal prefixes (e.g., 21.0.0.0/8, 100.0.0.0/8, 110.1.x.x/32s) into Area 0 as summary LSAs, and R10 uses the default to reach external destinations—this is normal stub behavior.
- OSPF adjacencies are stable (Full) on R2 and R10, and end-to-end host reachability across the network is healthy.

Affected component: None; OSPF stub/ABR operation is correct.
Mechanism/localization: Default LSA is confined to Area 4 by R2; no conflicting default or recursive summaries exist on R2/R1/R3.
Root cause: The suspicion stems from misinterpreting the stub default behavior; there is no summarization loop.
Corrective fix: None required. If a default is undesired in Area 4, remove “area 4 stub” (not recommended) or redesign area types; otherwise, leave as-is.
